Requirements:
- Stateless API instances that can scale horizontally.
- Durable asynchronous jobs for expensive OCR/analysis.
- Bounded worker concurrency, queue limits, backpressure and admission control.
- Managed PostgreSQL with appropriate high availability, backups and connection pooling.
- Private image storage, short-lived signed URLs, upload limits and retention controls.
- Secrets manager, TLS, least privilege and isolated development/staging/production environments.
- Health/readiness checks, graceful shutdown, rolling deployment and rollback.
- Monitoring for API, database, queue, OCR/AI provider and billing failures.
- Prefer a modular monolith initially. Split services only when measured scale or team boundaries justify it.
4. Android production requirements
Reliability
- Remove mock/demo responses from production builds.
- Use typed API models, explicit error states, lifecycle-aware ViewModels and non-blocking work.
- Recover from process recreation, interrupted uploads, timeouts and temporary offline conditions.
- Use bounded retries with exponential backoff and jitter; do not retry non-idempotent operations without idempotency.
- Preserve recoverable scan drafts without putting large bitmaps into saved instance state.
- Add crash reporting and performance monitoring with sensitive-data redaction.
Scan flow
1. Start scan and check server-side entitlement/quota.
2. Capture or import image; validate type, size and readability.
3. Use on-device OCR where practical; use server fallback only if needed and privacy-reviewed.
4. Preserve original OCR output.
5. Let the user review/correct extracted text.
6. Normalize and match ingredients; keep unmatched text visible.
7. Retrieve approved evidence and run deterministic rules.
8. Generate constrained explanation only from retrieved evidence.
9. Validate claims and schema; persist a versioned report.
10. Show findings, sources, unknowns and limitations.
11. Support retry, save, delete and feedback.
Mobile security
- Use platform-appropriate secure token storage; never log tokens.
- Enforce HTTPS and review Android network security configuration.
- Never trust client-supplied user IDs, roles, subscription status or quota counts.
- Review exported components, deep links, debug flags, backup rules and WebViews if present.
- Minimize permissions; test permission-denied paths.
- Scan dependencies and use supported versions.
- Obfuscation is not a security boundary.
Preserve the design palette: Smoky Black #11120D, Olive Drab #565449, Bone #D8CFBC, Floral White #FFFBF4. Test TalkBack, font scaling, contrast, touch targets, small screens and all loading/error states.
5. Backend requirements
API
- Version endpoints, e.g. /v1; validate schemas, content types, parameters and uploads.
- Authenticate and authorize every user-owned resource.
- Use stable error codes, correlation IDs, pagination and bounded response sizes.
- Set request timeouts, body limits and rate limits.
- Use idempotency keys for scan creation, analysis and billing-sensitive mutations.
- Redact sensitive fields from logs; never return stack traces or secrets.
- An upstream timeout must not become a safety finding.
Jobs and resilience
- Use durable jobs for expensive processing.
- Make jobs idempotent and safe to retry.
- Bound retries, concurrency, timeouts and external-provider spend.
- Define dead-letter and manual replay policies.
- Store job state so users can check progress or resume.
- Use backpressure/admission control instead of accepting unlimited work.
- Avoid duplicate reports, charges or quota consumption during retries.
- Gracefully degrade nonessential features when a provider is unavailable.
Ingredient-analysis integrity
- Separate ingredient identity, evidence, regulatory rules, user preferences and generated explanations.
- Review/approve evidence records before production use.
- Track source, organization, jurisdiction, effective date, review date and version.
- Use deterministic rules for regulatory status and preferences.
- Constrain AI to retrieved evidence; validate source IDs and claims.
- Defend against prompt injection in label text.
- If evidence is missing, return “insufficient information.”
- Save the evidence, rule, schema, prompt and model versions used by each report.
Billing
- Verify purchases server-side with the billing provider.
- Client entitlement values are never authoritative.
- Handle pending, active, grace, cancelled, expired, refunded and revoked states as applicable.
- Reconcile provider notifications and support restore purchases.
- Enforce quotas atomically on the server.
6. Database and privacy
- Use version-controlled migrations, foreign keys, unique constraints and measured indexes.
- Use transactions for report persistence and quota consumption.
- Use a bounded connection pool; additional API replicas must not overwhelm the database.
- Test query performance with realistic data volume.
- Separate runtime, migration, reporting and admin roles where feasible.
- Define retention separately for images, OCR text, reports, preferences, analytics, billing and audit logs.
- Provide data export/deletion where required. Deletion jobs must be retryable and auditable.
- Document legal retention exceptions and backup expiry.
- Encrypt and restrict backups; test restoration regularly.
- Define RPO (acceptable data loss) and RTO (target recovery time).
- Test database failover and application recovery.
7. Capacity objective: 10,000+ concurrent users
“10,000 concurrent users” is not precise enough to size infrastructure without request rates, scan frequency, image sizes, traffic bursts and latency objectives.
Required workload profiles
- 10,000 active clients using the app.
- Read-heavy browsing/history traffic.
- A burst of 10,000 scan submissions.
- Sustained requests at an agreed requests-per-second rate.
- Large image uploads.
- OCR/AI latency, concurrency and quota limits.
- A partial dependency failure while traffic continues.
For context, 10,000 users each making one API request every 10 seconds average about 1,000 requests/second. This is an example, not a sizing guarantee.
Controls
- Horizontally scale stateless API instances behind a load balancer.
- Keep expensive work out of synchronous request threads.
- Use durable queues, bounded workers, queue limits and backpressure.
- Scale workers based on queue depth/age while enforcing provider and cost limits.
- Apply per-account and per-IP abuse controls.
- Cache safe public/reference data; never publicly cache private reports.
- Bound database connections and measure slow queries.
- Add timeouts/circuit breakers for external services.
- Display queued/progress status instead of losing a scan when work is delayed.
- Do not claim a scan is complete just because a job was accepted.
Do not invent server sizes or replica counts before load testing the actual implementation.
8. Service objectives and load testing
Agree on targets before testing:
- Monthly availability objective.
- p95/p99 latency for reads and scan submission.
- Acceptable server error rate.
- Queue wait time and end-to-end scan completion time.
- RTO/RPO.
- Maximum acceptable cost per scan.
- Security release thresholds.
Use k6, Gatling or Locust with synthetic accounts and non-sensitive test data.

Test:
1. Smoke test.
2. Ramp to 10,000 virtual users.
3. Sustained load.
4. Sudden spike.
5. Simultaneous scan burst.
6. Long soak test for leaks and queue growth.
7. Slow/unavailable OCR, AI, queue and database failover.
8. Recovery and queue drain.
9. Abuse: oversized uploads, invalid tokens, repeated requests and quota bypass.
Record:
- Test script/version, environment and configuration.
- Virtual users, request mix, requests/second and completed scans/second.
- p50/p95/p99 latency, error rates and timeouts.
- CPU, memory, network and disk.
- DB connections, slow queries, locks and saturation.
- Queue depth, oldest-job age, retries and dead-letter count.
- Provider concurrency, rate-limit errors and cost.
- Recovery time and data-integrity results.
Pass condition: the agreed workload is sustained while latency/error objectives are met, with no cross-user data exposure, data corruption, uncontrolled queue growth or runaway provider spend. Fix bottlenecks and rerun. Do not exceed third-party quotas or terms during testing.
