Logs · metrics · alerts. 2. Define what “10,000 concurrent users” actually means
The system must be sized for the workload, not just the number of installed apps.
Scenario	Required engineering response
10,000 users online	Efficient authentication, connections, caching and database access
10,000 users browsing simultaneously	Scalable API instances and bounded database connections
10,000 users starting scans together	Queueing, admission control, worker autoscaling and backpressure
10,000 expensive AI analyses at once	Provider quota management, concurrency limits, retries and cost controls
Sudden traffic spike	Load shedding, graceful degradation and automatic recovery

For example, 10,000 users each making one request every 10 seconds produce roughly 1,000 requests per second. If all 10,000 submit a scan simultaneously, the scan-processing pipeline must absorb that burst without overwhelming the database or AI provider.

For ScanSafe.AI, I would use on-device OCR where practical, asynchronous backend analysis, and a durable job queue. The app should show scan progress and recover from temporary failures instead of freezing or losing the user's work.
3. Production security requirements
Authentication and authorization

Secure session handling, short-lived access tokens where appropriate, refresh-token protection, server-side ownership checks, role-based access for administrators, and protection against unauthorized access to another user's reports.



Secrets and data protection

Never embed backend, database, or AI-provider secrets in the APK. Use managed secrets, TLS, private object storage, encrypted backups, and carefully controlled access to personal preferences and scan images.



API and abuse protection

Validate every request, enforce upload limits, rate-limit expensive operations, protect against injection and broken access control, and prevent users from bypassing free scan quotas or subscription entitlements.



Database and recovery

Use managed PostgreSQL with high availability, connection pooling, tested backups, point-in-time recovery where supported, safe migrations, and tested restoration procedures. A backup is not sufficient until restoration has been tested.



Monitoring and incident response

Monitor errors, latency, resource saturation, queue delays, failed scans, database connections, and AI-provider limits. Set alerts and establish procedures for responding to incidents.



Security should be tested against the OWASP Mobile Application Security Verification Standard (MASVS) and OWASP API Security risks. No single checklist can guarantee complete security.
